7 a xsd:decimal,
        xsd:integer ;
    owl:sameAs 7 .

"0.1" owl:sameAs "0.1" .

"All 24 statements of what fuels and starves the five hindrances and seven awakening factors, each as a nutriment node (object + kind of attention + effect). The opening statement (1.2) anchors both lists." owl:sameAs "All 24 statements of what fuels and starves the five hindrances and seven awakening factors, each as a nutriment node (object + kind of attention + effect). The opening statement (1.2) anchors both lists." .

"Everything else. The text is not held; the pairings need checking against an edition before they can be marked verified." owl:sameAs "Everything else. The text is not held; the pairings need checking against an edition before they can be marked verified." .

"Five pairings of a jhāna factor against a hindrance (citing the Peṭakopadesa)." owl:sameAs "Five pairings of a jhāna factor against a hindrance (citing the Peṭakopadesa)." .

"Its 12 statements of what fuels the five hindrances and seven awakening factors (2-6, 9-15). They match SN 46.51 sections 1-12 (identical English; Pali differs only in punctuation and one spelling variant), so they were added as a second citation on the same claims, not as new claims." owl:sameAs "Its 12 statements of what fuels the five hindrances and seven awakening factors (2-6, 9-15). They match SN 46.51 sections 1-12 (identical English; Pali differs only in punctuation and one spelling variant), so they were added as a second citation on the same claims, not as new claims." .

"Most of the dialogue: wisdom and consciousness (2-6), feeling and perception (7-11), the goal of wisdom and conditions for right view (12-14), states of existence (15-17), what the first absorption is (18), the five faculties (21-22), vital forces and death vs cessation (23-25), and the releases of the heart (26-37)." owl:sameAs "Most of the dialogue: wisdom and consciousness (2-6), feeling and perception (7-11), the goal of wisdom and conditions for right view (12-14), states of existence (15-17), what the first absorption is (18), the five faculties (21-22), vital forces and death vs cessation (23-25), and the releases of the heart (26-37)." .

"Nothing doctrinal. The question (x.1) and conclusion (x.4) of each section restate the claim and are not cited separately." owl:sameAs "Nothing doctrinal. The question (x.1) and conclusion (x.4) of each section restate the claim and are not cited separately." .

"One definition: 'Unification of the mind is immersion' (12.2). Found while checking an eval answer; it corrected decision D4, which had wrongly called this equation an interpretation." owl:sameAs "One definition: 'Unification of the mind is immersion' (12.2). Found while checking an eval answer; it corrected decision D4, which had wrongly called this equation an interpretation." .

"One segment (10.2) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning the Buddha's teaching." owl:sameAs "One segment (10.2) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning the Buddha's teaching." .

"One segment (36.2), recorded as a contested use of the word 'dhamma' and deliberately left without a sense." owl:sameAs "One segment (36.2), recorded as a contested use of the word 'dhamma' and deliberately left without a sense." .

"Only the first absorption: its five factors (19.2-19.3), and that it gives up the five hindrances and possesses those five factors (20.2-20.4)." owl:sameAs "Only the first absorption: its five factors (19.2-19.3), and that it gives up the five hindrances and possesses those five factors (20.2-20.4)." .

"The banner simile and the recollection of the Buddha, the teaching and the Saṅgha as a remedy for fear." owl:sameAs "The banner simile and the recollection of the Buddha, the teaching and the Saṅgha as a remedy for fear." .

"The first line of the verse ('All things are not-self') as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning any phenomenon." owl:sameAs "The first line of the verse ('All things are not-self') as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning any phenomenon." .

"The rest of Dhammadinnā's answers: substantial reality, its origin, cessation and the practice leading there (2-5); grasping and the aggregates (6); substantialist view (7-8); the noble eightfold path and the three spectrums of practice (9-11); the bases, prerequisites and development of immersion (12.3-12.5); the three processes (13-15); the cessation of perception and feeling (16-21); the three feelings, their underlying tendencies and their counterparts (22-29)." owl:sameAs "The rest of Dhammadinnā's answers: substantial reality, its origin, cessation and the practice leading there (2-5); grasping and the aggregates (6); substantialist view (7-8); the noble eightfold path and the three spectrums of practice (9-11); the bases, prerequisites and development of immersion (12.3-12.5); the three processes (13-15); the cessation of perception and feeling (16-21); the three feelings, their underlying tendencies and their counterparts (22-29)." .

"The rest of the verse, and the rest of the Path chapter (Dhp 273-289)." owl:sameAs "The rest of the verse, and the rest of the Path chapter (Dhp 273-289)." .

"The setting and the challenge from wanderers of other religions (1.1-3.1); the four fire similes (5, 8, 11, 14) that illustrate the advice; the repeated statements after each simile (6, 9, 12, 15.1-15.3)." owl:sameAs "The setting and the challenge from wanderers of other religions (1.1-3.1); the four fire similes (5, 8, 11, 14) that illustrate the advice; the repeated statements after each simile (6, 9, 12, 15.1-15.3)." .

"The simile framing the teaching: as the body is sustained by food, so the hindrances and the awakening factors are sustained by fuel (1.2-1.3, 7, 8, 16). There is no pattern for similes yet." owl:sameAs "The simile framing the teaching: as the body is sustained by food, so the hindrances and the awakening factors are sustained by fuel (1.2-1.3, 7, 8, 16). There is no pattern for similes yet." .

"The six sets of six (sense fields, objects, consciousnesses, contacts, feelings, cravings) and the not-self argument built on them." owl:sameAs "The six sets of six (sense fields, objects, consciousnesses, contacts, feelings, cravings) and the not-self argument built on them." .

"The timing advice: which awakening factors it is the right or wrong time to develop when the mind is sluggish (4.1, 7.1) or restless (10.1, 13.1), the reason given for each, and 'mindfulness is always useful' (15.4)." owl:sameAs "The timing advice: which awakening factors it is the right or wrong time to develop when the mind is sluggish (4.1, 7.1) or restless (10.1, 13.1), the reason given for each, and 'mindfulness is always useful' (15.4)." .

"The whole teaching on the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. Its section on the five hindrances (knowing when each is present, how it arises, how it is given up) bears directly on this ontology and should come next." owl:sameAs "The whole teaching on the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. Its section on the five hindrances (knowing when each is present, how it arises, how it is given up) bears directly on this ontology and should come next." .

"Two segments (5.3, 6.8) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning an object of the mind." owl:sameAs "Two segments (5.3, 6.8) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning an object of the mind." .

"dhp279:1" owl:sameAs "dhp279:1" .

"editor; matches Sujato's rendering 'qualities'" owl:sameAs "editor; matches Sujato's rendering 'qualities'" .

